According to the [RDF Turtle file specification](https://www.w3.org/TR/turtle), some IRIs can be specified as relative IRIs against a base IRI. The [specification of relative IRIs](https://www.w3.org/TR/turtle/#relative-iri) is as follows:

> [IRIs](http://www.w3.org/TR/rdf11-concepts/#dfn-iri) may be written as relative or absolute IRIs or prefixed names. Relative and absolute IRIs are enclosed in '<' and '>' and may contain [numeric escape sequences](https://www.w3.org/TR/turtle/#numeric) (described below). For example <http://example.org/#green-goblin>.
>
> Relative IRIs like <#green-goblin> are resolved relative to the current base IRI. A new base IRI can be defined using the '@base' or 'BASE' directive. Specifics of this operation are defined in [section 6.3 IRI References](https://www.w3.org/TR/turtle/#sec-iri-references)

So for example, if you ingest the triples in the example in the specification:

```
@base <http://example.org/> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
