While searching for a workaround of https://github.com/kuzudb/kuzu/issues/2293, I noticed following difference to Neo4j:
```
CREATE NODE TABLE T(id INT64, PRIMARY KEY(id));
CREATE REL TABLE KNOWS (FROM T TO T);
CREATE (t:T {id:0}), (t1:T {id:1}), (t)-[:KNOWS]->(t1);
```
```
kuzu> OPTIONAL MATCH (:T {id: 0})-[e]->() WITH e MATCH ()-[e]-() DELETE e;
Error: Binder exception: Bind relationship e to relationship with same name is not supported.
```
In contrast, Neo4j understands, that `e` in the last `MATCH ()-[e]-()` is a variable reference to a previously matched, optional existing relationship `e` and does indeed find it.

Not sure, if this is still super relevant after above issue fix. But if I understood correctly, one goal is to have feature parity with Neo4J's Cypher, so it might be worth to document remaining differences.

In Neo4j
```
OPTIONAL MATCH (a)-[b:KNOWS]->(c) return a,b,c;
```
returns `NULL` values for non-existent nodes and relationships (amongst other metadata)
```
    "_fields": [
      null,
      null,
      null
    ],
```

With Kuzu statements
```
CREATE NODE TABLE T(id INT64, PRIMARY KEY(id));
CREATE REL TABLE KNOWS(FROM T TO T);
OPTIONAL MATCH (a)-[b:KNOWS]->(c) return a,b,c;
```
, the API returns
```
[
    [
        {
            "_label": "T",
            "_id": {
                "offset": 18446744073709551615,
                "table": 18446744073709551615
            },
            "id": null
        },
        {
            "_src": {
                "offset": 18446744073709551615,
                "table": 18446744073709551615
            },
            "_dst": {
                "offset": 18446744073709551615,
                "table": 18446744073709551615
            },
            "_label": "KNOWS"
        },
        {
            "_label": "T",
            "_id": {
                "offset": 18446744073709551615,
                "table": 18446744073709551615
            },
            "id": null
        }
    ]
]
```
, which seems too bloated. What about using `NULL` to represent non-existence of entities, instead of returning pseudo-entities with `id: null` and computed internal id?

Also in CLI the output is misleading:
```
kuzu> OPTIONAL MATCH (a)-[b:KNOWS]->(c) return a,b,c;
----------------------------------------------------------
| a             | b                      | c             |
----------------------------------------------------------
| {, _LABEL: T} | ()-{_LABEL: KNOWS}->() | {, _LABEL: T} |
----------------------------------------------------------
(1 tuple)
```
Better would be:
```
----------------------------------------------------------
| a             | b                      | c             |
----------------------------------------------------------
| NULL | NULL | NULL |
----------------------------------------------------------
